'''Antüko''' (Mapudungun pronunciation: {{W|IPA|[aν.tuˈko.t͡ʃe]}}) is a [[Regions of Balisca|region]] of [[Cerdanya]] within the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]]. It is bounded by [[Pigüe]] to the west, and the [[Willi Mapu]] [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|indigenous nation]] to the south. It is included in all definitions of [[Rural society in Balisca|Rural Balisca]].

The administrative seat and largest city in Antüko is [[Las Alemáns]].

After the securing of independence in 1818, a lack of economic attractions and successive [[Baliscan Civil War|civil wars]] provided few incentives for immigration to the region, with the exception of German immigration to northern Cerdanya, who were contracted by the Cerdanyan government and played a substantial role in the development of the [[W:Logging industry|logging industry]]. German immigration included the founding of Las Alemáns in 1843, and German traders played a significant role in the development of the Cerdanyan interior and for trade on the [[W:Argentine Sea|Baliscan (Argentine) Sea]].

